    description: "FILED 2026-09-09 from WI-kurug (T0a of INV-linub's drain). Record:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/t0_sizing_09092026/kurug/.\n\nTHE ONE GENUINE R2 (ambiguous-name) FAILURE IN THE WHOLE FOUR-LANGUAGE STUDY.\nerlang came back 14/14, cpp's single miss was a duplicate catalogue row, and\nelixir's nine misses were two other mechanisms entirely. haskell is the only\nlanguage where the F3 gate refuses a call for exactly the reason the route\npredicts.\n\nMEASURED on servant, in files the analyzer demonstrably read:\n    Prelude.print   6 sites   servant-conduit/example/Main.hs:88, :91\n                              servant-pipes/example/Main.hs:90 and others\n    shape: `print err` / `print x` -- BARE, no module qualifier anywhere on the\n    line, and `print` is in haskell's own `ambiguous_names`.\n\nSo the edge (if emitted) carries no module_hint and no qualified callee name,\nand `_lookup_named_entry` correctly refuses it. This is the F3 gate working as\ndesigned on evidence that genuinely is not there -- the C shape, at haskell\nscale.\n\nSIZE, stated honestly: haskell declares only 10 R2-only rows out of 276, and\nonly 3 were eligible on servant (called, in an analyzed file). This is a SMALL\ncell. It is filed because a route that fails is a route that fails, and because\nINV-linub cannot be satisfied for haskell while a catalogued primitive with\nlive call sites reaches nothing.\n\nREJECTED FROM THIS COUNT BY READING, so the 1 is not really 2: the instrument\nalso flagged `Control.Concurrent.yield` at servant-pipes/example/Main.hs:67,\nbut `P.yield m` is PIPES' yield through the alias P. The import gate passed it\nbecause the file does import Control.Concurrent; distinguishing it mechanically\nwould need alias-origin tracking, and it is rejected here by reading the line.\n\nTHE PRE-REGISTRATION PREDICTED UNDECIDED FOR HASKELL on the grounds that the\neligible population might be empty. It was not empty. Prediction refuted, in the\ndirection of there being something there.\n\nFIX DIRECTION, unresolved and deliberately not chosen here: haskell's module\ncontext lives in the IMPORT LIST, not at the call site (`import\nNetwork.Socket.ByteString (recv)` then bare `recv`). That is the same shape as\nthe go import-block route that satisfies INV-linub's module_hint disjunct\n(INV-tanom) and the same shape as the C `#include` route WI-lajus shipped\ntoday. A per-file import map feeding the module slot is the precedented\nmechanism; whether it is worth building for 10 rows is a sizing question for\nwhoever takes it, and WI-lajus is the cautionary case -- stamping too broadly\nsuppressed a permissive fallback and MOVED VERDICTS THE WRONG WAY."  # 881e
    fields: {}  # 881e
