- op: discuss  # 3f6c
  at: '2026-09-11T05:38:11Z'  # 3f6c
  by: agent  # 3f6c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3f6c
  clock: 3  # 3f6c
  nonce: 3f6c  # 3f6c
  message: "DONE. Record: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lokun_09112026/ (PLAN.md pre-registered\nbefore any outcome number; Addendum A appended).\n\nTHIS ITEM'S FILED REPRO, RE-RUN. `print err` / `print x` reproduces at the\nfiled lines, and `Prelude.print` was absent from servant's boundary map while\n`Prelude.putStrLn` carried 8 chains -- same module, same files, same bare call\nshape. It now reaches the catalogue with 3 `logging` chains.\n\nTHE MECHANISM IS SHARPER THAN \"the F3 gate refuses it for want of a module\".\n`gate_named_entry` ends:\n    if ambiguous_names and name in ambiguous_names: return None\nso with the `external` sentinel an AMBIGUOUS name reaches nothing while an\nUNAMBIGUOUS one still matches through the permissive short-name path. The\nbaseline table IS the diagnostic: putStrLn 8 chains, print 0.\n\nSCOPE IS EXACTLY THAT ASYMMETRY, and it is narrower than the item implies.\nOnly a name in `ambiguous_names` is stamped -- 8 names, derived from the\ncatalogue rather than listed. An unambiguous name already classifies, so\nstamping it buys NOTHING and switches module filtering on for a call that was\nworking. That is this item's own cautionary case (WI-lajus) applied in advance\ninstead of after a regression, and three unambiguous Prelude names\n(`putStrLn`, `writeFile`, `getLine`) are pinned by test to stay `external`.\n\nTWO ROUTES, everything else abstains:\n  (a) an unqualified `import M (name)` list -- the route THIS ITEM FILED.\n  (b) the name's home module being `Prelude` with nothing in the file claiming\n      otherwise -- where the filed INSTANCE actually lives, since Haskell\n      imports Prelude implicitly and it appears in no import line at all. The\n      item's \"module context lives in the IMPORT LIST\" framing does not cover\n      its own example.\nA `qualified` import is skipped deliberately (it does not put the bare name in\nscope). An explicit claim beats the Prelude route, so\n`import Data.Text.IO (readFile)` beside `import Prelude hiding (readFile)`\nnever yields `Prelude`.\n\nMEASURED on servant (PYTHONHASHSEED=0, blob asserted BEGIN and END):\n  total_io_edges 50 -> 53; haskell chains 46 -> 49; distinct (kind, primitive)\n  15 -> 16. NEW: `logging Prelude.print` 3. **LOST: 0. CHAIN COUNTS CHANGED: 0**\n  (`Prelude.putStrLn` stayed at exactly 8). Every verify-claims verdict\n  byte-identical -- predicted, since `logging` was already violated.\n\nALL THREE CHAINS READ BACK AGAINST SOURCE, AND THEY CORRECT MY OWN RATIONALE.\nThe sites are servant-conduit:88/91, servant-pipes:90/93 and\nservant-machines:82/85 -- the filing named TWO files, there are THREE. Each\ncarries `import Prelude ()` + `import Prelude.Compat`, the base-compat idiom,\nwhose EMPTY import list SUPPRESSES the implicit Prelude: `print` arrives\nre-exported from `Prelude.Compat`, not from the implicit Prelude route (b)\nreasons about. The stamp is still correct -- `Prelude.Compat` re-exports\n`Prelude.print` verbatim and the catalogue rows a function's HOME module, not\nwhichever module re-exported it -- but the justification encoded is not the one\noperating at these sites. Route (b) is better read as \"the home module is\nPrelude and nothing in this file claims otherwise\". Written into the code\ndocstring and pinned by two tests rather than smoothed over.\n\nSIZE, honestly: 3 chains. The item predicted a small cell and it is one. What\nit closes is the last live R2-route failure across cpp/elixir/erlang/haskell,\nso INV-linub's haskell row no longer has a catalogued primitive with live call\nsites reaching nothing."  # 3f6c
